Make an anchor parameter's bare form take the body leaf's uniqueness rule

Dev accepted, in these words: "Accepted, build it" — the recommendation being that `anchorParameter` in
`NameResolution.rewrite` apply the same judgment as `short`: a local binding keeps its authored spelling, an
own-package name is written bare only when `short` would shorten it, everything else stays in full. One engine
change, no definition or committed Scala change, one new ResolutionRecordTest case.

Why. The resolver had two rules for spelling a name short. A BODY leaf goes through `short` (2026-10-21,
narrowed with the corpus sweep): the bare name only when the simple name is unique across the ontology AND
the host vocabulary — Decoder and Encoder in the Dracon domain stay in full in their own package for that
reason. An ANCHOR's type parameter — the tuple inside `Primal((Gaze, Distance))` — went through
`anchorParameter`, which wrote an own-package name bare with no such check. The sentient surfaces (5f0e023)
exposed the asymmetry: the loader had refused bare `Distance` as ambiguous against `draco.base.Distance`, the
accepted correction spelled `domains.sentient.Distance` in full in Percept's and Waypoint's headers, and the
resolver then undid it — `resolve` rendered the own-package anchor parameter bare again, so the COMPACT form
(resolve, then re-read) of either definition failed on the ambiguity the correction had removed. No gate
caught it: the two-stage loader only reads, and the example gate generates unscoped.

MEASURED before recommending, over all 183 definitions of main and mods: 33 anchor parameters are bare
names. Thirteen resolve to a definition in the anchor's own package — DomainType and TypeDictionary
(DomainOntology's Dictionary), TypeName and TypeDefinition (TypeDictionary's), XML, Dracon twice (Value's and
Dracon's own Format), Gaze twice (Percept, Waypoint), Percept and Effect (Ego's Holon), World twice (Consumer's
and Provider's Actor) — and every one of the thirteen is unique across the ontology and is no host binding's
alias or target name, so the uniform rule writes each bare as before. Six are already in full (Dracon, Text
twice, ScalaTarget, DrakeTarget cross-package; `domains.sentient.Distance` twice), and `short` keeps a
non-unique name in full, so Percept and Waypoint stay as 5f0e023 spelled them. The remaining twenty are
formals (S, T, F) or host leaves (Double, Enumeration, String), which the local binding or the vocabulary
decides and neither rule shortens. So no committed JSON or Scala file moves; the only observable change is
that the compact form of Percept and Waypoint now equals the committed JSON and reads back.

Built as measured. `anchorParameter` gains one conjunct: the own-package bare form is written only when
`short(full, local) != full`. REJECTED: dropping the own-package restriction so an anchor parameter could take
the record's short form — the 2026-10-21 rule keeps anchors readable by the unscoped companions, and this
point does not touch it.

ResolutionRecordTest +1 (five cases): over the example caller scope (ExampleScope's declarations and
bindings), Percept and Waypoint carry the tuple `{"(,)": ["Gaze", "domains.sentient.Distance"]}`, resolve to
themselves (Gaze bare, Distance in full), compact to the committed JSON, read back from the compact form,
generate the same unscoped and scoped, and generate the committed companion; Ego's `Holon(Percept, Effect)`
and TypeDictionary's `Dictionary(TypeName, TypeDefinition)` compact to their committed JSON, the unique
own-package anchor parameter still bare.

